{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"26e98ab0-0fe5-49e7-9a6a-85b1e5888171","title":"Uniform circular motion and centripetal force"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"26e98ab0-0fe5-49e7-9a6a-85b1e5888171","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Uniform circular motion means constant speed around a circle. The velocity still changes because its direction changes.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Uniform circular motion"},{"op":"figure","id":"motion","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-4,4.5],"y":[-4,4],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"path","center":[0,0],"r":3},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"center"},{"kind":"point","id":"object","at":[3,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"object"},"stroke":"dashed","label":"r"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"v=\\frac{2\\pi r}{T}=\\text{constant}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"26e98ab0-0fe5-49e7-9a6a-85b1e5888171","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Velocity is tangent to the circle, perpendicular to the radius. Here the object moves counterclockwise, so its velocity points upward.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"velocity","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"v_arrow","from":{"at":"object"},"to":[3,2.2],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":3,"start":15,"end":55,"arrow":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[2.65,0],[2.65,0.35],[3,0.35]],"stroke":"solid"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"tangent","anchor":{"el":"speed","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\vec v\\perp\\vec r"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"26e98ab0-0fe5-49e7-9a6a-85b1e5888171","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Changing direction requires inward acceleration. The net force points toward the center, not along the velocity.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"inward","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"force_arrow","from":{"at":"object"},"to":[0.7,0],"arrow":true,"label":"F net"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.6,-0.65],"text":"inward","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"accel","anchor":{"el":"tangent","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"a_c=\\frac{v^2}{r},\\qquad F_{\\text{net,in}}=m\\frac{v^2}{r}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If speed doubles while mass and radius stay fixed, what happens to the required inward net force?","expected_gist":"It quadruples because the force is proportional to speed squared."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"26e98ab0-0fe5-49e7-9a6a-85b1e5888171","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Centripetal describes the net inward force, not an extra force. Tension, gravity, or friction can supply it. Doubling speed requires four times the force.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"scaling","on":"motion","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[0,-3.6],"text":"2v → 4F","size":"normal"}]},{"op":"text","id":"source","anchor":{"el":"accel","side":"below","gap":0.12},"content":"Inward force: tension, gravity, friction..."},{"op":"equation","id":"scalelaw","anchor":{"el":"source","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\frac{F(2v)}{F(v)}=\\frac{(2v)^2}{v^2}=4"}],"checkpoint":null}}
